WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.180
There’s a new trailer for the next Dune&nbsp;
movie. It reveals the Emperor of the Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.360
Shaddam Corrino. It shows surprising tensions&nbsp;
between Chani and Paul. And it explores the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.340
story’s theme of political and religious&nbsp;
messiahs. So what happens in the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.660
and how does it compare to the&nbsp;
Dune book? This video has no big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.820
spoilers for the movie – just some background&nbsp;
information to prepare us for Dune Part Two.&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.600
The trailer begins with the sun and moons&nbsp;
of Arrakis. This sun is called Canopus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.900
which is a real star – it’s the second-brightest&nbsp;
star in Earth’s night sky, so Canopus has mythic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:45.480
significance in many real-world cultures, and&nbsp;
is referenced in many other sci fi stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:52.560
Arrakis has two moons, and the larger moon has a&nbsp;
shape on it that looks like a human hand. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.240
the smaller moon has a shape that looks like&nbsp;
a kind of kangaroo-mouse found on Arrakis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.480
called Muad’Dib. We saw the Muad’Dib mouse&nbsp;
three times in the first movie. The Fremen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.880
admire Muad’Dib for its ability to survive in the&nbsp;
desert. So there’s a constellation named after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.460
mouse that points north – Muad’Dib is a guide&nbsp;
to the Fremen. And as we see in this trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:21.480
Paul takes Muad’Dib as his name, as he too&nbsp;
becomes a guide and a leader to the Fremen.&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:28.200
Paul says “This world is beyond cruelty”. The&nbsp;
environment of Arrakis is harsh, so to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.320
the Fremen learned to withstand suffering, and to&nbsp;
make “brutal judgments” . Like in the first movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.900
when the Fremen were gonna kill Paul and Jessica&nbsp;
to take their water . In the desert, brutality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:46.500
and suffering aren’t good or bad, they’re not&nbsp;
cruel, they are just.. necessary to survive – so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:53.100
in that sense, Arrakis is “beyond cruelty”.
We see Paul’s hand on the sand, which is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.800
like a vision Paul had in the first movie.&nbsp;
Dune explores how people change environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.980
and how environments change people. So how&nbsp;
will this harsh environment change Paul?&nbsp;

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:12.240
We see a Harkonnen gunship firing, maybe attacking&nbsp;
some Fremen. The ships in these movies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:18.240
organic, curving shapes inspired by nature – like,&nbsp;
this ship was based on the shape of an armadillo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:25.140
This harvester looks like a sinister squid or&nbsp;
spider. This gunship was inspired by bubonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.060
plague infections, and gangrenous tumors,&nbsp;
as though the Harkonnens are a sickness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.120
an infection on this land. Director Denis&nbsp;
Villeneuve calls the Harkonnens “invaders”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:42.360
“colonizers”, and “predators”. The bulging&nbsp;
shape of the hovering gunship also looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:49.860
like the fat levitating Baron Vladimir Harkonnen.
We see the Baron and Beast Rabban with a Harkonnen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:55.800
army. There’s more sinister, hungry-looking&nbsp;
Harkonnen architecture behind them, reminiscent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.880
of the old H.R. Giger Dune concept art. This place&nbsp;
might be Carthag, the Harkonnen city on Arrakis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.480
which is 200km from Arrakeen, the smaller older&nbsp;
city where the Atreides settled. it looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.768
the Baron has some.. floating globes attached to&nbsp;
him – maybe this is a medical device, like a sci&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.768 --> 00:03:21.240
fi IV drip, cause remember the Baron was poisoned&nbsp;
in the first movie, maybe he’s still recovering.&nbsp;

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:27.300
In the first movie, the Baron told Rabban&nbsp;
to kill all the Fremen – maybe that’s what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.700
this army is for. But killing all Fremen won’t&nbsp;
be easy – we learned in the first movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.740
the Fremen are much more numerous and more&nbsp;
powerful than the Harkonnens realise . And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.560
now Paul and Jessica have joined the Fremen.
Paul says the Fremen have fought the Harkonnens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.600
for decades – because the Harkonnens ruled&nbsp;
Arrakis for eighty years before the Atreides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:54.360
took over. But the Atreides have fought Harkonnens&nbsp;
for centuries. Cause these two families have hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.720
each other for ten thousand years, ever since&nbsp;
the Battle of Corrin. This battle is when House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.720
Corrino rose to power – the ancestors of the&nbsp;
Emperor Shaddam and Princess Irulan established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.960
their empire. And after this battle, an Atreides&nbsp;
got a Harkonnen banished for cowardice, starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:20.040
a blood feud that continues to this day. The&nbsp;
Baron wants to finally win this ancient conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:26.040
by exterminating the Atreides. He also wants to&nbsp;
cover up that the Emperor helped the Harkonnens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:31.080
with his Sardaukar soldiers – cause if the other&nbsp;
houses find out that the Emperor helped attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.660
the Atreides, the houses would all turn against&nbsp;
Shaddam . So that might be why the Harkonnens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:43.020
burn all these corpses, destroying the evidence.
We see Paul reflecting on the death of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.600
father Leto. Paul now has Leto’s ducal&nbsp;
signet ring. Because in the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.480
Yueh took this ring from Leto, and got it to&nbsp;
Paul. Cause the ring represents the leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:01.320
of House Atreides. With Leto dead, Paul is now&nbsp;
the Duke, and the ten thousand year Atreides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:07.260
dynasty is now his responsibility. Can he&nbsp;
defeat the enemies who killed his father?&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:13.980
We see a religious painting of Leto. Cause&nbsp;
the prophecy of the Lisan al-Gaib, or Madhi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.800
suggests that Paul is a messiah who will save&nbsp;
the Fremen , and Paul’s parents Leto and Jessica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:26.820
are also considered sacred. In the book, Paul has&nbsp;
visions of a shrine with his father’s skull – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:32.520
you can see the teeth of Leto’s skull atop the&nbsp;
shrine here. And in the visions, this skull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:39.060
shrine feeds the wild fanaticism of a holy war .&nbsp;
So Paul’s parents are not just important to Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:45.000
personally, his family has profound religious and&nbsp;
political significance to the fate of this planet.&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:51.360
We see a fight scene where Paul and Chani&nbsp;
wait to ambush some Harkonnen soldiers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.520
the desert. Paul grabs an enemy sword&nbsp;
and kills a Harkonnen with a *cool spin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:04.920
move*. Jessica kills a Harkonnen with a rock.
We also see this huge battle, with Chani fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.320
against the Emperor’s Sardaukar. This shot looks&nbsp;
like a vision Paul had in the first movie, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:16.380
himself fighting Sardaukar. We see Paul shouting&nbsp;
Chani’s name – he looks distressed to see his love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:24.000
Chani swept up in the violence that he foresaw.
In the book, we don’t see Paul or Jessica or Chani&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.180
fighting random soldiers. We do hear about some&nbsp;
fights and battles that happen off-screen – like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:35.760
it’s mentioned that Paul goes on raids with the&nbsp;
Fremen , and Chani has a badass moment. But we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.440
don’t see these fights happen in the book.&nbsp;
Because the book is focussed on thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:47.340
conversations, visions, plans, and politics. The&nbsp;
book is less about sword fights, and more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:54.420
battles of the mind. Of course, it’s difficult&nbsp;
to show thoughts on a screen – and that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:07:01.500
Dune is so hard to adapt into a movie. So Dune&nbsp;
Part Two adds new fight scenes and explosions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.440
to make this an “action-packed” “war movie”,&nbsp;
a visual spectacle. But hopefully it won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.740
lose the thoughtful tone, and the meaning of&nbsp;
the book – cause Dune is not about violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:19.620
Dune is about the power of the mind.
In the trailer, Paul and Chani attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:24.060
a spice harvester. Maybe they’re&nbsp;
disrupting Harkonnen spice mining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:29.940
like at the start of the first movie.
Chani uses a big weapon to blow up an ornithopter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:35.580
which is similar to a bit in the book – Paul&nbsp;
gives the Fremen rockets to use against enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:41.340
aircraft. You can see this explosion contained&nbsp;
within the thopter’s shield for a moment. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.200
this weapon shoots a slow projectile that&nbsp;
can pass through a shield before exploding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:52.380
like the slow bombs we saw in the first movie.
We see Fremen climbing under the spice harvester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:59.400
maybe to plant explosives, or steal spice. Paul&nbsp;
jumps onto Chani, maybe to protect her from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:05.520
gun we saw in the first trailer. Remember, in&nbsp;
the Dune universe, people usually don’t use guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:11.580
because most soldiers have shields that block&nbsp;
bullets, making guns useless. But Fremen don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:17.580
use shields, because on Arrakis, shields attract&nbsp;
sandworms, and shields are often disabled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.680
electrical storms. So in the book, the Harkonnens&nbsp;
realise that since Fremen don’t use shields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:28.860
they can use guns and lasguns against them . But&nbsp;
the Fremen also have their own advantage – like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.920
they know this land, and can use&nbsp;
guerilla tactics against the Harkonnens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:41.340
striking then disappearing into hidden caves&nbsp;
. And the Fremen now have Paul on their side.&nbsp;

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.080
In the trailer, a Fremen tells Paul&nbsp;
“You're not welcome here”. Cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.360
there are many different Fremen tribes on&nbsp;
Arrakis. Stilgar’s group may accept Paul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:58.560
but other Fremen may not. The Dune books explore&nbsp;
some unusual and extreme Fremen subcultures, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:06.480
the legendary Iduali of Jacurutu . So how will&nbsp;
Paul and Chani deal with Fremen who oppose him?&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:13.020
Chani goes to translate the Fremen’s words, but&nbsp;
Paul interrupts and says he already understands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:19.560
thanks. It’s a moment of sarcastic dry&nbsp;
humour from Paul. Cause in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:26.280
Paul is usually serious, stressed, haunted by&nbsp;
visions, high on spice, walking a tightrope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.800
through a political and religious storm. But&nbsp;
it looks like this movie tries to humanise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:38.580
Paul with some more light-hearted dialogue.
In the trailer, Paul reunites with Gurney – Paul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:44.160
and Gurney are close friends in the book. In the&nbsp;
first movie, it seems like Duncan is Paul’s best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:50.400
friend, but in the book, Gurney is his favourite.
Gurney now has longer hair, and Paul has blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.520
eyes, which is caused by exposure to spice&nbsp;
over time. So it looks like months or years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:03.060
have passed. Paul and Gurney have both struggled&nbsp;
to survive on this alien world separate from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:09.240
friends and familiar faces, so it’s emotional&nbsp;
when these guys reunite. After their time apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:15.120
Paul and Gurney are confronted by how much&nbsp;
they’ve both changed . This reunion is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.520
also a nice scene in the 80s David Lynch Dune&nbsp;
movie, with Gurney played by Patrick Stewart.&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:27.660
Later, we see Gurney facing Beast Rabban. Gurney&nbsp;
was once a slave of the Harkonnens – Rabban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:34.440
destroyed Gurney’s family, and gave Gurney the&nbsp;
scar on his face, so Gurney wants revenge . The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:39.600
Dune book never tells us exactly what happens&nbsp;
to Beast Rabban in the end . But it looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.360
the movie might give us a dramatic clash&nbsp;
between these old enemies. This could be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:49.740
good opportunity to show off Gurney’s fighting&nbsp;
skills, because in the book, Gurney is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:56.280
of the most skilled warriors in the universe .
It also looks like Paul faces Rabban at one point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:03.300
So there’s a bunch of new Rabban stuff in this&nbsp;
movie, and Rabban does not look ready. Director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:08.760
Villeneuve say “there’s something touching about&nbsp;
Rabban”, he wants to please the Baron, but he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:14.700
“not very intelligent”, he doesn’t have the brain&nbsp;
for this war. That said, there are hints in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:20.340
book that Rabban may be smarter than he seems,&nbsp;
so they might also play with that in the movie.&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:27.480
Stilgar is in charge of a group of Fremen,&nbsp;
he’s the naib of Sietch Tabr . He is a strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:34.620
intelligent , leader. But now he’s becoming a&nbsp;
follower of Paul. In the trailer, Jessica asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:41.160
“Do you believe in Paul?”, and Stilgar says “There&nbsp;
are signs”. Stilgar is impressed that Paul rides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:47.280
this huge sandworm. And that Paul killed Jamis in&nbsp;
the first movie. In the trailer, he raises a knife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:53.760
for Paul fanatically. And says “I don't care what&nbsp;
you believe! I believe”. It looks like Stilgar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:59.760
believes that Paul is the Lisan al-Gaib, the&nbsp;
prophesied leader who will save the Fremen . And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.500
we see strength and passion in Stilgar’s faith.&nbsp;
But it’s also kinda tragic and wrong. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:13.920
the Lisan al-Gaib prophecy was made up by the Bene&nbsp;
Gesserit to manipulate the Fremen. Paul says it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:21.600
“not a prophecy”, just a “story”. Jessica says it&nbsp;
gives the Fremen hope, but Paul says “That's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:27.688
hope”. Paul does have real power, he has visions,&nbsp;
he may be the Kwisatz Haderach gigabrain from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.688 --> 00:12:32.580
Bene Gesserit breeding program, but the prophecy&nbsp;
of him saving the Fremen is a lie. Dune explores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:40.200
what it means to believe, the impact of religion&nbsp;
on cultures and on people . So how will faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:45.180
change Stilgar and his relationship with Paul?
Gurney says it’s good that the Fremen think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:50.160
Paul is a messiah. He says to use&nbsp;
that power. But Paul is reluctant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:57.120
because he has visions of a terrible holy war –&nbsp;
an unstoppable tide of violence sweeping across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:04.320
the universe with him at its centre. Gurney says&nbsp;
Paul is scared of losing control. But Paul says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:10.860
he’s scared of gaining control – Paul worries that&nbsp;
his choices could bring disaster . And it’s hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:16.620
cause Paul’s visions are uncertain, they’re always&nbsp;
changing and shifting. Like, in the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:22.920
Paul had a vision of Jamis being his friend, and&nbsp;
a vision of Chani stabbing Paul, and those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:29.520
didn’t happen – Paul’s visions don’t always come&nbsp;
true, they’re just “possible futures” . So in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.780
book, Paul is haunted by these terrible&nbsp;
visions, trying to use them to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:40.080
but also trying to prevent the holy war . You can&nbsp;
see the visions in Dune as a metaphor for our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:47.340
hopes and fears, plans and predictions. There are&nbsp;
dangers in trying to make our dreams come true.&nbsp;

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:55.080
Paul asks if Chani believes in him. Cause when&nbsp;
Paul plays the role of a leader and messiah to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.080 --> 00:14:03.360
the Fremen, Chani looks angry. Chani says “This&nbsp;
prophecy is how they enslave us”. She sees the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:08.160
truth, that the prophecy is a lie meant to&nbsp;
exploit the Fremen. And Chani says she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:13.920
not fighting for Paul, she fights for her people.&nbsp;
And this is a change from the book. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:19.500
Chani doesn’t question the prophecy, she’s a&nbsp;
mostly passive character who just supports Paul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:25.680
The book does explore how wrong it is that the&nbsp;
Atreides use prophecy to manipulate the Fremen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:30.420
but that’s mostly done through the thoughts&nbsp;
of Paul and Jessica . It looks like the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:36.720
makes this theme more explicit by having Chani&nbsp;
loudly reject the exploitation of her people.&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:45.300
But at the same time that she criticises Muad’Dib&nbsp;
the messiah, Chani loves Paul. Paul says “I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:52.320
love you as long as I breathe”, and Paul and&nbsp;
Chani cry together. Crying is a powerful sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:58.560
act to the Fremen, because crying wastes moisture,&nbsp;
when Fremen culture is about conserving moisture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:05.760
Crying is called “Giving water to the dead”.&nbsp;
So this an intense emotional scene. Paul says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:12.720
“I will do what must be done” – what terrible&nbsp;
choices or sacrifices must he make that he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:19.380
Chani weep for the dead? And how will they resolve&nbsp;
the tension of Chani opposing Paul’s religion? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:25.380
does it feel when your boyfriend is space Jesus?
There’s also a shot of Chani thumping the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:31.680
with her fist – it’s like in the first movie when&nbsp;
Kynes thumps the ground to attract a sandworm.&nbsp;

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:39.420
We see visions of Paul’s mother Jessica. If Paul&nbsp;
is the messiah, what is her destiny? Director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:45.360
Villeneuve says Jessica “lost everything”. She’s&nbsp;
still grieving the death of her partner Leto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:51.600
and the loss of her home on Caladan. But she’s&nbsp;
also carrying new life – remember Jessica is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:58.980
pregnant – Paul had a vision of her baby in the&nbsp;
first movie. So Jessica struggles to adapt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:05.280
Arrakis, but she focusses on protecting her son&nbsp;
and unborn child. In the book she believes they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:12.600
must use the Fremen to secure her family’s future&nbsp;
. So we see more of this ritual, with this liquid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:18.180
the Water of Life. We see Jessica gasp, with&nbsp;
blue eyes, and see this old Fremen woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:24.840
Ramallo. This is called the Ceremony of the Seed&nbsp;
– it’s one of the great Fremen mysteries, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:32.340
it is transformative – what will Jessica become?
Then we see the Padishah Emperor of the Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:38.400
Shaddam Corrino, played by Christopher Walken.&nbsp;
Twenty years ago, Walken was in a music video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.840
that referenced Dune – the lyrics “Walk&nbsp;
without rhythm and it won't attract the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:48.720
worm” refers to Dune’s sand walk. So it’s&nbsp;
funny that now, Walken’s in a Dune movie.&nbsp;

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:54.780
In all the big Dune adaptations, the Emperor is&nbsp;
played by older men. But that’s not how he looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.780 --> 00:17:00.180
in the book. In the book, Shaddam is seventy-two&nbsp;
years old, but he looks like he’s thirty-five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:06.480
– presumably cause Shaddam takes spice, cause&nbsp;
spice extends your life, makes you live longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:13.800
and look younger. In the book, Shaddam is slim and&nbsp;
elegant, with a thin face and cold eyes, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:20.460
has red hair. Apart from the hair, Shaddam looks&nbsp;
a lot like Paul’s father Leto, because they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:26.940
cousins – the Atreides and Corrinos are related.&nbsp;
Leto has dark olive skin in the book , and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:33.600
Shaddam’s grandson is “saturnine”, so Shaddam&nbsp;
probably also has dark olive skin in the book.&nbsp;

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:40.500
So Christopher Walken does not look like the 35&nbsp;
year old dark-skinned red head described in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:47.400
book. But what his personality? Who is Shaddam?
As the Emperor of the Universe, Shaddam appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:54.600
to be the most powerful man alive. In the book&nbsp;
he wears military Sardaukar uniforms to show his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.600 --> 00:18:01.860
strength. He acts confident, arrogant, wrathful&nbsp;
and demanding, like a man who must be obeyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:08.580
But Shaddam is not as powerful he seems.
Cause the Emperor’s power is limited by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:14.940
the Bene Gesserit. Shaddam’s wife Anirul is&nbsp;
a Bene Gesserit, all his children are Bene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:21.060
Gesserit trained. And the Bene Gesserit control&nbsp;
Shaddam’s reproduction – they don’t let him have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:27.000
a son. The Bene Gesserit plan to put a Bene&nbsp;
Gesserit on the throne after Shaddam. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:32.940
Spacing Guild also have influence on Shaddam.
So while Shaddam acts like he’s all-powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:39.600
he doesn’t have control of his succession, or&nbsp;
over his own reproduction. Irulan writes “he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:47.820
a man fighting constantly to escape the bars of an&nbsp;
invisible cage”. So all Shaddam’s pretentions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:55.680
power, the pomp and pride and fury, is impotent,&nbsp;
and pathetic. You get a sense of that helplessness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.680 --> 00:19:01.800
from Shaddam’s slumped posture in the trailer&nbsp;
here. He and his daughter Irulan play a board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:04.560
game with little pyramids. [like a mashup of&nbsp;
chess and go and Cones of Dunshire]. The Dune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:10.740
book mentions a game called “pyramid chess”, or&nbsp;
“cheops”, named after the real Egyptian pharaoh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:17.160
who built the great Giza pyramid. Pyramid chess&nbsp;
is a “nine-level” game about putting the king in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:23.820
check and putting the queen at the apex. Which&nbsp;
is a nice metaphor for Shaddam – Shaddam is like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:29.580
a king in chess, because he’s not a powerful&nbsp;
piece – it’s the queen, the Bene Gesserits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:36.900
who have the real power. So we might imagine that&nbsp;
Irulan is winning this game against her father.&nbsp;

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:42.960
They play in a beautiful sunlit garden.&nbsp;
This is probably the Corrino planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:49.620
Kaitan. This comfortable leafy environment is&nbsp;
a contrast to the harsh deserts of Arrakis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:54.180
To the Fremen, this garden would be&nbsp;
an unimaginable luxury, a paradise.&nbsp;

00:19:54.180 --> 00:20:01.500
In the book, Shaddam indulges in the “pomp”&nbsp;
and opulence of court – he flaunts his wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:09.180
with useless luxury and fancy events . He sits&nbsp;
a massive throne carved of fiery quartz from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:15.240
“Jewel Planet”. And this luxury is important,&nbsp;
because the book explores how indulgence can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:21.180
make you weak. The 1984 Dune movie captured&nbsp;
this opulence well, but in this new trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:28.560
we don’t see any opulence – Shaddam and his&nbsp;
attendants wear simple plain clothes. Hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:34.500
other scenes will rizz up Shaddam’s drip.
In the trailer, Irulan tells Shaddam that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:40.380
Paul is still alive. And Shaddam says to&nbsp;
“Deal with this prophet”, which perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:46.140
leads to Irulan’s investigation plotline&nbsp;
hinted in the first trailer. None of that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:52.020
in the book – it looks like the movie creates&nbsp;
new scenes to explore Shaddam and Irulan more.&nbsp;

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:59.580
In the trailer, Shaddam says Paul’s father&nbsp;
Leto was “weak”. Which is odd. Because Shaddam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:05.520
attacked Leto because he was worried about Leto’s&nbsp;
strength. The book says that Shaddam feared Leto.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:12.180
And also respected him. Shaddam wished that Leto&nbsp;
had married his daughter Irulan, he wished Leto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:18.720
could’ve been his son-in-law. So when Shaddam&nbsp;
calls Leto weak, maybe he just says that out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:25.920
spite and envy and frustration, projecting his&nbsp;
own weakness onto Leto. Shaddam is a complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:33.720
character, a man of terrible wrath and superficial&nbsp;
charm and sad impotence. Hopefully Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:40.380
Walken captures these layers to Emperor Shaddam.
At the end of the trailer, we see this reflective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:46.980
spherical orb, which looks like the Imperial&nbsp;
spaceship in the first Dune movie. This orb may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:53.280
Shaddam’s ship – and the structure beneath it is&nbsp;
this metal tent glimpsed in the big battle scene.&nbsp;

00:21:53.280 --> 00:22:01.080
We see more of Feyd-Rautha Harkonnen fighting in&nbsp;
an arena. We see Feyd’s two blades – in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:06.600
Feyd has a black glove holding a long&nbsp;
knife, and a white glove holding a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:07.822
short poisoned knife. It’s surprising that&nbsp;
Villeneuve didn’t include that black-and-white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.822 --> 00:22:10.620
imagery in this black-and-white scene.
But the Baron looks down on his nephew Feyd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:16.920
and says “Show me who you are”. Cause the fights&nbsp;
in Dune are all about revealing your potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:24.660
through focus and challenge. What is Feyd capable&nbsp;
of? And what are the Baron’s plans for Feyd?&nbsp;

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:30.900
We see Feyd fighting Paul – another&nbsp;
black-and-white duality between a Harkonnen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:37.980
beast and a mindful Atreides. This shot of Feyd&nbsp;
raising his knife matches the shot of Paul raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:44.100
his knife. In some ways, Feyd and Paul are alike&nbsp;
– they are both the young heirs to ancient houses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:50.220
And they both are products of the Bene Gesserit&nbsp;
breeding program. In the book, the Bene Gesserit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:56.100
planned for Jessica to have a daughter not a&nbsp;
son, and that daughter was going to marry Feyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.100 --> 00:23:01.740
and their child together was meant to be the&nbsp;
super-powerful Kwisatz Haderach. But instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:08.280
Jessica had a son, and now that son is fighting&nbsp;
the man who he was kinda meant to marry. &gt;Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:15.900
is a disaster for the Bene Gesserit plan. What&nbsp;
happens these two powerful opposites clash?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:22.440
Feyd’s knife at Paul’s face looks like the gom&nbsp;
jabbar and the hunter-seeker at Paul’s face – a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:28.860
deadly challenge. We see Paul thump his chest&nbsp;
with his fist, like how Jamis thumped his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:36.240
fist – Paul remembers the lessons of the Fremen.
In the trailer, Paul raises his knife to a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:42.540
crowd of Fremen. And he shouts “Long live the&nbsp;
fighters”. He says this in the Fremen language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:48.480
that was invented for these movies by David J.&nbsp;
Peterson. &gt;Peterson also created the Valyrian and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:54.720
Dothraki languages for Game of Thrones. But in the&nbsp;
Dune book, these words are different – the phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.720 --> 00:24:01.380
meaning “Long live the fighters”, is “Ya hya&nbsp;
chouhada”, which is loosely based on real Arabic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:07.620
The author Frank Herbert seems to have got this&nbsp;
phrase from a 1961 article about the Algerian War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:14.040
when Algeria won independence from the French. The&nbsp;
article describes a leader raising a hand over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:19.320
crowd, and they roar “Ya Hya Chouhada”, which&nbsp;
the article says is Arabic for “long live the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:26.460
fighters”. The Dune book, published just a few&nbsp;
years later, has a very similar scene – Stilgar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.460 --> 00:24:32.580
raises a knife over a crowd, and they roar&nbsp;
“Ya hya chouhada”. &gt;Frank even uses the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:39.720
words as the article – “throng” and “roar”. This&nbsp;
translation and anglicisation of the Arabic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:44.940
not very accurate, but the point is that this&nbsp;
line in the book is inspired by real Arabic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:51.420
and by a real war between Muslim freedom fighters&nbsp;
and European colonists, which has similarities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:57.120
the Fremen fighting the Harkonnens. Frank was also&nbsp;
inspired by a book about Muslims in the Caucasus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:02.700
fighting the Russian Empire. And by Lawrence&nbsp;
of Arabia with the Bedouin. Frank drew from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:08.880
many different Arabic, North African, and Islamic&nbsp;
cultures to create the Fremen, and the Dune book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:15.240
makes this explicit in their language. The Fremen&nbsp;
use lots of Arabic and Islamic terms including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:22.320
Ramadan , “bourkas”, sharia , and the hajj .&nbsp;
The new movies include some Arabic words – like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:28.800
Lisan al-gaib, Shai-Hulud, Madhi, and Muad’Dib&nbsp;
are all loosely based on Arabic . But the movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:34.860
also make changes to remove Arabic words.&nbsp;
Like, in the book, Paul has visions of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:40.920
jihad – but the movies just call this “holy war”.&nbsp;
Changing the words for “long live the fighters”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:48.300
is another example of these movies removing and&nbsp;
minimising the Arabic and Islamic roots of Dune.&nbsp;

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:55.500
So Dune Part Two makes changes from the book.&nbsp;
It adds more fight scenes and explosions. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.500 --> 00:26:01.620
adds new scenes with Irulan and Shaddam, and&nbsp;
Rabban. And creates a tension between Chani and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:08.520
Paul. But this trailer does focus on the central&nbsp;
theme of the book – the danger of political and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:14.640
religious messiahs – so hopefully this movie&nbsp;
will capture the heart and meaning of Dune.&nbsp;

